.P

RX Signalling System                                                   4510

Enter the number of the Signalling System that is desired for the  receiver 
of  this  mode. Signalling Systems are set-up under the  Signalling  System 
Configuration Screen.  

If there is no Signalling System for this mode enter "00". The only types of
systms that you may assign are Quik-Call II Advantage (QCII) and Generic
Advantage (Generic).

You may have only ONE signalling system per mode. This means that if you have
a Rx Signalling System, then you may NOT have a Tx Signalling System AND the
Simple Option Board field is automatically DISABLED and NOT available.
.P

TX Signalling System                                                   4511

Enter  the  number  of  the  Signalling  System that  is  desired  for  the 
transmitter of this mode. Signalling Systems are set-up under the  
Signalling System Configuration Screen.  

If there is no Signalling System for this mode enter "00". The only types of
systems that you may assign are Generic Advantage (Generic).

You may have only ONE signalling system per mode. This means that if you have
a Tx Signalling System, then you may NOT have a Rx Signalling System AND the
Simple Option Board field is automatically DISABLED and NOT available.
.P


BUSY CHANNEL LOCKOUT                                                   4512

Busy  Channel Lockout may be enabled by choosing "Y".  When enabled,  the 
radio will be 'polite' to other users on the channel.  In other words,  the 
radio will not let you hear conversations of other users and will not allow 
you  to transmit on top of another user.  The only calls you will hear  are 
those  with  your PL code,  even if you press  the  monitor 
button.  If  you  press PTT on the radio while there is a  carrier  on  the 
channel, the radio follows this protocol:

          a. If the carrier has your PL, then you are allowed to transmit
             just like normal.
.P
          b. If the carrier has no PL or the wrong PL, then you will hear
             a  busy  tone while you hold PTT.  Release PTT and  try  again
             later.

If you choose "N", then the radio will transmit and receive like a normal 
two-way radio.     
.P


VOX                                                                    4513

This field determines if the Voice Operated Transmission is enabled or
disabled.  If enabled, VOX requires an accessory headset that has a Mic
audio live at all times.  

The radio will sense the presence of the headset each time the radio is 
turned on.  You may, therefore, defeat VOX by removing the headset and 
cycling the power switch.

When channel scan is enabled, the radio's software must decide as to which
way VOX will behave.  This decision will supercede all other mode 
specifications regarding VOX.
.P
BATTERY SAVER                                                          4514

This field sets the rate of the Battery Saver feature.  The possible choices
for this field are Short, Medium, Long or Off.  

The following is a summary of characteristics for the three rates:

    RATE      RX On Time    Sleep Time     Improved Capacity

    Short      50 ms           50 ms             8%
    Medium     50 ms          150 ms            15%
    Long       50 ms          250 ms            20%
 
.P
POWER                                                                  4515

This field sets the  power level.   The choices  are high power, or low
power. Your radio is  calibrated at the factory for high  power only. If
you change this field to low power, you need to align the "Low" relative
value setting in the TX Power Adjustment screen  in order to obtain your
desired low power output.  Your  particular   model  may  be  calibrated
to different power levels  due to local regulatory requirements  Consult
your Service Manual for more information.

.P

SIMPLE OPTION BOARD                                                    4516

Configures the radio to use a simple type option board and enables the
option board on the current mode.

Enabling (set to "Enable") allows the useage of third party boards such as
scramblers.

You may have only ONE signalling system per mode. This means that if you have
the Simple Option Board field ENABLED, then you may NOT have a Rx Signalling
System AND you may NOT have a Tx Signalling System.
.P

SCAN LIST NUMBER                                                       4521

This  field indicates  the number of the  scan list that is  assigned to be
used with this mode.

.P

PRIORITY SAMPLING RATE                                                 4522

When the channel scan is stopped on a non-priority channel, it periodically 
"looks back" at the priority channel for activity.  The rate at which  this 
"look  back"  occurs  is  the priority sample  rate.   The  are  two  rates 
available,  slow  and fast.  Slow is 1.5 seconds between "look  backs"  and 
Fast is 0.5 seconds between "look backs".  Slow is the default.  To  change 
the sample rate use the up and down arrow keys to select the rate.

Note : Earlier radio software versions do not support this feature! 
.P

HOME REVERT MODE                                                       4524

This field sets the Home Revert Mode for this channel.  

If  the TalkBack  feature is  enabled, then  the  radio  will act  in the 
following  manner.  If you hear activity on  a mode, then  the radio will 
stop on this active mode until the  hang timer expires.  During this hang 
time any transmission will occur on this active mode.  In all other cases, 
the radio will revert to the Home Mode.  

If  TalkBack is  disabled,   then the  radio  will always  revert  to the 
preprogrammed Homd Mode for transmissions,   regardless  of hang time.
.P
HOME REVERT MODE (cont)

The  scan will  default with TalkBack enabled when a scan list is created.

The Home Revert Mode  can be specified as any conventional channel in the 
radio.  A good practice  is to  leave this set at the default priority 1.
.P

ACTIVE CHANNEL TONE                                                    4526

This field will determine if the Active Channel Tone is enabled or disabled.
If enabled, Active Channel  Tone will allow the  user to rotate the channel
selector to  find  the last active  channel.   When the channel is found, a
single tone  will  be heard.   The tone  will remain  associated with  that 
channel until the radio is powered off/on, or the PTT is pressed.
.P

PRIORITY CHANNEL TONE                                                  4527

This field  determines if the Priority Channel Tone is enabled or disabled.
If enabled, a tone will be produced whenever the  radio scans to  and stops
on a priority channel.  This alerts the operator that a priority message is
being  listened to.  It is suggested that this tone be turned off if there 
is heavy use of RapidCall calling features on the priority channel.
.P

SCAN ACTIVATE TONE                                                     4528

This field determines if the Scan Activate Tone is enabled or disabled.  If
enabled, a tone will be  produced when the rotary  selector  is turned to a
scan list  position.  This is helpful to the user when the radio is worn on
a belt.
.P

PHONE SIGNALLING SYSTEM                                                4529

Enter the number of the Signalling System that is desired for use when
performing phone dialing operations on this mode.
.P
TX INHIBIT                                                             4530

This feature is only available when the channel has a CSQ receive code.  
When enabled, this feature will not allow the radio to transmit while a
carrier is present on the channel.  A busy tone will be heard in such a case.
.P
LOCAL/DISTANCE                                                         4531

Local/Distance is a feature that affects radio sensitivity and interference
rejection. Use the UP/DOWN arrow keys to select a value for this feature.

Use LOCAL in areas of dense radio usage (suburbs and major cities). LOCAL
improves IM performance dramatically, but loses some sensitivity.

Use DX (distance) when in an area where radio is decoding weak signals 
(rural areas).
.P

MODE UTILITY                                                           4580

The  MODE  UTILITY  screen is used for  adding,  deleting,  or  moving  the 
location  (order)  of  Conventional  modes.    Modes  may  also  be  copied 
(duplicated), including scan lists (Mode-Slaved only).

The  F8  (EXECUTE)  function key is used to execute the  changes  once  all 
information has been entered.

The  GET/SAVE functions must be used to program the changes into the radio, 
as is required for all CHANGE/VIEW modifications.
.P

UTILITY (Default =  Copy Mode)                                         4581

Use  the  UP/DOWN arrow keys to select the desired UTILITY  function: Copy 
Mode or Move Mode.

The  F8  (EXECUTE)  function key is used to execute the  changes  once  all 
information has been entered.

The  GET/SAVE functions must be used to program the changes into the radio, 
as is required for all CHANGE/VIEW modifications.
.P

MODE TYPE                                                              4582

Mode Type is Conventional and cannot be changed.
.P

INSERT AFTER MODE                                                      4583

Enter the mode number that the mode is to be INSERTED AFTER.

After entering this number and pressing Enter,  the mode name for that mode 
will  be  displayed.   Use  the name to verify that the proper  number  was 
entered.
.P

NEW MODE NAME                                                          4584

Enter the 8-character alpha-numeric MODE NAME.   The Mode Name will default 
to "Mode nn", where nn = mode number.  If Mode Names are disabled, you will 
not be able to change this field. 

Each  mode  may be assigned a Name to facilitate ease  of  operation.   The 
[Mode] rocker button is used to scroll forward or backward through the list 
of modes programmed in the radio.

Since  the mode number is required for direct keypad mode selection via the 
[Home] button for keypad models,  using the mode number as part of the mode 
name is desirable, but not required.
.P

MODE TO COPY FROM                                                      4585

Enter the Mode Number TO COPY FROM.  Any valid conventional mode number may 
be entered.

After entering this number and pressing Enter,  the mode name for that mode 
will  be  displayed.   Use  the name to verify that the proper  number  was 
entered.

Frequencies  (Rx,  Tx,  T/A),  squelch type,  squelch codes,  and all  mode 
options  will  be copied.   You may choose to copy the  channel  scan  list 
(mode-slaved  only)  or  to enter a new one.

This  field is required for the Copy Mode function;  it is optional for the 
Add Mode function.
.P

COPY SCAN LIST (Default = N)                                           4586

Use the UP/DOWN arrow keys to select Y to copy the channel scan list (mode-
slaved only) or N for a blank list.

If  the  copied  list  is full (16 modes),  the new mode  will  assume  the 
priority of the copied mode and the copied mode will not be part of the new 
list. 

Otherwise,  the new mode will assume the priority of the copied  mode,  and 
the copied mode will be a non-priority mode on the new list.
.P

MODE TO COPY TO (Default = Blank)                                      4587

Enter  the Mode Number TO COPY TO.

After entering this number and pressing Enter,  the mode name for that mode 
will  be  displayed.   Use  the name to verify that the proper  number  was 
entered.

Frequencies  (Rx,  Tx,  T/A),  squelch type,  squelch codes,  and all  mode 
options  will  be copied.   


.P

MODE TO DELETE                                                         4588

Enter the Mode Number TO DELETE.  

After entering this number and pressing Enter,  the mode name for that mode 
will  be  displayed.   Use  the name to verify that the proper  number  was 
entered.

CAUTION:  After  the F8 (EXECUTE) function key has been pressed,  the  mode 
          will be deleted and may not be recovered.  However, if you make a 
          mistake  AND the data has not been programmed or saved  (GET/SAVE 
          functions), you may re-read the codeplug or original archive file 
          and start over.
.P

MODE TO MOVE                                                           4589

Enter  the  Mode Number TO MOVE.   Any valid conventional mode  number  may 
moved.  

After entering this number and pressing Enter,  the mode name for that mode 
will  be  displayed.   Use  the name to verify that the proper  number  was 
entered.


The mode and all the releated parameters will be moved to the new position.


.P
PHONE MEMORY DIALER                                                    4600

This screen allows the user to create a list of phone numbers.  Up to nine
16-digit phone numbers can be entered on this screen.

.P
SYSTEM                                                                 4700

This  screen  allows  you to  configure one  or  more  signalling  systems.  
These  signalling systems will be used by the Call List and Mode screens to 
determine when and where decode or encode.
.P

TYPE                                                                   4702

When this screen is displayed the cursor is placed on the TYPE data  field.  
The   available  system  types  are None, QCII, and Generic. The system 
types are selected by using the Up/Down arrow keys.  None  implies  that all
of the signalling systems in the  radio  are deactivated.

NOTE: Since QCII and Generic TYPEs can function only if a Radius Advantage
Option Board is installed, these TYPEs are only available for System 01.
.P

ID                                                                     4703

QCII :
  QCII ID corresponds to a combination of tones A, B, C, and/or D.  
  It requires  at two least two unique tones.  The defaults are 358.6 Hz 
  (tone A) and  903.2 Hz (tone B).   The range is from 304.0 to 2470.0 Hz.
  Don't be alarmed if the frequency that you type in changes by a few Hertz.
  This change can occur because the program must calculate  the nearest
  frequency that  the radio  can  understand.   You will find that the error
  will always be  within the acceptable  industry bandwidth.
.P


GROUP ID                                                               4704

The  MDC1200 ID is a three hex digit number common to a group  of  radios 
and ranges from 000 to EEE.



.P

CALL FORMAT                                                            4705

There are 9 different choices for this field. The choice determines the 
sequence in which this radio needs to see the QCII tones for an individual
or group call. The Up/down arrow keys will scroll through the choices. The
following is a list of these choices:

             A-B  Tone A followed by Tone B.
         A-B/A-C  Tone A followed by Tone B or Tone A followed by Tone C.
         A-B/C-B  Tone A followed by Tone B or Tone C followed by Tone B.
      A-B/Long B  Tone A followed by Tone B or Long Tone B.
      A-B/Long C  Tone A followed by Tone B or Long Tone C.
  A-B/A-C/Long C  Tone A followed by Tone B or Tone A followed by Tone C or
                  Long Tone C.
    A-B/Long B/C  Tone A followed by Tone B or Long Tone B or Tone C.
.P
CALL FORMAT (cont) 

A-B/A-C/Long B/C  Tone A followed by Tone B or Tone A followed by Tone C or
                  Long Tone B or Tone C.
     A-B/A-D/C-D  Tone A followed by Tone B or Tone A followed by Tone D or
                  Tone C followed by Tone D .

In this table the first sequence is considered an "individual call" while
the other sequences would be considered "group calls." Fore example:
For choice A-B/Long B, an A-B sequence would be an "individual call" while
a Tone B or a Long Tone B would be a "group call". 
.P

CALL TYPE                                                              4706

There are three different choices for this field.  The Up/Down arrow keys 
will scroll through the following choices :  Call Alert,  Call Alert/Voice, 
and  Voice  Selcall.   Call Alert leaves call indication at  the  receiving 
radio.    Call  Alert/Voice  leaves  a  call  indication  and  unmutes  the 
loudspeaker  of the receiving radio.   Voice Selcall unmutes loudspeaker on 
receiving radio but does not leave any call indication.  MDC-1200 does  not 
allow Voice Selcall call type since Voice Selcall is an inherent feature of 
all MDC-1200 signalling systems.
.P

PL/DPL REQUIRED                                                        4707

The Up/Down arrow keys will allow the choices of either "Y" or "N".   "Y" 
implies the correct PL/DPL is required to recieve data.  "N" implies PL/DPL 
is  not  required  to receive data.  In most systems you  may  choose  "N", 
however,  when  you  share  a channel with other users who  have  the  same 
signalling type then you should choose "Y". Choosing "Y" on shared channels 
will ensure that you don't accidentally receive a call which is directed to 
another user. 

Note :  Some existing Quik Call systems require PL stripping, and 
therefore it is recommended that an "N" be chosen in these cases. 
.P

ALERT TONE RESET                                                       4708

Alert Tone Reset only applies if the Call Type is CALL ALERT or CALL ALERT
W/VOICE.  This determines how the audible alert tone will be reset.  The
choice of AUTOMATIC means that the alert tone will be automatically reset
for you.  The choice of MANUAL means that there will be repeating alert
tones every 10 seconds that must be manually reset by pressing any button.
.P

CONVERSATION RESET                                                     4709

Conversation Reset only applies to a Call Type of CALL ALERT W/VOICE.  When
a call alert is received you may wish the radio remain in CSQ operation for
a follow-up voice message or so that you may hold a brief conversation.  At 
the end of the conversation, you may also want the radio to return to its
previous squelch condition.  If you choose MANUAL RESET, then the radio 
will reset the squelch condition immediately after the operator presses the
monitor button.  If the choice of AUTO RESET is selected, then the radio 
will wait for seven seconds after a carrier loss and/or radio transmission
before resetting the squelch condition.
.P

ACK PRETIME                                                            4711

  Systems   that  use Call Alert require that the radio  acknowledge  (ACK)  
that the  Call Alert has been received.  ACK Pretime is the delay from when  
the  radio  receives  a  Call Alert to when it starts  the  ACK   sequence.   
The default ACK pretime is 0.5 seconds.  If a different pretime is required 
for your  system move the cursor to the ACK Pretime data field and   change  
the time  using  the up and down arrow keys.  The range can be from 0.2  to  
2.0 seconds in 0.1 second steps.
.P

HORN/LIGHTS                                                            4712

When a Call Alert is received, an external output may be activated that can
be used to control a vehicle's horn or lights. Horn/Lights may be either
selectable, permanent, or not availabled. If Horn/Lights are selectable,
then they may be defeated by the front panel OPT Button. If they are
permanent, they cannot be defeated without an external switch in the relay
cable. If they are not available, then they can never be activated.

NOTE: The selectable feature is available ONLY if the OPT Button has been
      programmed for Selectable H/L.

      The permament feature is available ONLY if the OPT Button has NOT been
      programmed for Selectable H/L.
.P
HORN/LIGHTS DELAY                                                      4713

When Horn/Lights is activated by the operator, Call Alerts will activate
the external alarm for six seconds. Horn/Lights Delay will delay the 
activation of the external alarm to allow the operator time, if he is in
the vehicle, to respond to the Call Alert before the external alarm is
activated. If the operator does respond during the delay time the external
alarm will not be activated. The default time is 10.0 seconds. If a different
delay time is required move the cursor to the Horn/Lights Delay field and
enter the desired delay. Use the UP/DOWN arrow keys to select a delay of
0, 5.0, 10.0, or 15.0 seconds.
.P
SIGNALLING SQUELCH                                                     4714

  This  feature can either be enabled "Y" or disabled "N" using the Up/Down 
arrow  keys.  If this feature is enabled "Y",  then one of the control but
tons  can  be used to put the radio into  signalling  squelch.   If  this 
feature  is  disabled "N",  then signalling squelch is not  activated.  The 
default is "N".

  Signalling  Squelch is similar to Private Line.  When enabled,  the radio 
will  only unmute for Call Alerts and Voice Selcalls.  Like  Private  Line, 
Signalling  Squelch  can  be  disabled by pressing the  monitor  button   
You   will   find  that Signalling Squelch is  automatically  ENABLED  when 
channel scan is on.    
.P

DOS HOLDOFF                                                            4715
  Data   Operated   Squelch  (DOS)  is  used with  data signalling  systems 
(such  as  MDC-1200)   to    mute    the  data  transmissions   to   reduce  
operator  annoyance.   DOS  attempts  to  mute  the  speaker  when data  is 
present  on  the channel.    DOS Holdoff keeps  the speaker muted   for   a  
preset period  of time from when carrier is  detected   by    the   squelch 
circuit.    This  allows the DOS time to detect any data  activity, further 
reducing the annoyance of data being heard from the speaker. 

  The  default  holdoff  is 0.5 seconds.    If a different holdoff time  is 
required  for   your system  move the cursor to the DOS Holdoff data  field 
and  change the   time using the Up/Down arrow keys.  The range can be from 
0.0   to  4.3  seconds  in  0.1 second  steps.     
.P


PTT ID                                                                 4716

  If you want your unit id to be sent on each Push-to-talk (PTT),  then set 
this field to "PRE", "POST", or "NONE".  "PRE" sends the id at the press of 
PTT.  "POST" sends the id upon release of PTT. If  you choose "NONE",  then 
the id will not be sent during PTT.     

.P

PTT SIDETONE                                                           4717

  PTT   sidetones   are  used when PTT ID is  activated.    Sidetones   are  
heard  through the speaker when the operator first presses the  PTT.   They 
are  used  to  tell  the  operator   that he cannot  speak  until  the   ID  
has   been transmitted.  There are two sidetones available in the  Radius.  
The  PTT  ID Sidetone  is a continuous tone heard from PTT until the ID has  
been  sent.  The second tone, PTT Short Sidetone, is a short beep after the 
ID  has   been sent.    Both  tell  the operator when he is  permitted   to  
speak  into  the microphone.   One,  both  or no Sidetones maybe activated. 
They   can  be activated  by moving the cursor to the desired sidetone  and 
using the Up/Down arrow keys to choose either "Y" or "N" to either activate 
or deactivate the sidetones. 

.P

PTT SHORT SIDETONE                                                     4718

  PTT   sidetones   are  used when PTT ID is  activated.    Sidetones   are  
heard  through the speaker when the operator first presses the  PTT.   They 
are  used to  tell  the  operator  that he cannot yet speak until  the   ID  
has   been transmitted.  There are two sidetones available in the  Radius.  
The PTT  ID Sidetone  is a continuous tone heard from PTT until the ID  has  
been  sent.  The second tone, PTT Short Sidetone, is a short beep after the 
ID  has   been sent.    Both  tell  the operator when he is  permitted   to  
speak  into  the microphone.   One,  both  or no Sidetones maybe activated. 
They   can  be activated  by moving the cursor to the desired sidetone  and 
using  the  Up/Down arrow keys to choose either "Y" or "N" to  activate  or 
deactivate the sidetones. 

.P
PRETIME                                                                4719
  The    pretime    is  the  delay from the start  of  transmit  (when  the 
operator pushes PTT) to when  the data signalling begins.    This delay  is 
required   in  order  to  allow   the  radio    to   completely   open  the 
communications  path  through   repeaters  or  other   system   components.    
The  default   pretime   is 0.5 seconds.    If  a  different   pretime   is  
required  for your  system move the cursor  to  the Pretime data field  and 
change the  time using the Up/Down arrow keys.   The range can be from  0.1 
to 4.2 seconds in 0.1 second steps.

.P
FIXED RETRY WAIT                                                       4720

  When  emergency  is  activated  by  the  operator  the  radio  expects an 
acknowledge from the base station.  If no acknowledge is received then  the 
radio   will repeat the emergency sequence after a delay.  The radio always 
waits a random time greater than twice the Pretime.  The additional time is 
the  Fixed Retry Wait time.  Fixed Retry Wait is the time the  radio  waits 
between emergency tries.  The default  is 0.0  seconds (STAR default is 8.1 
seconds).   If  a  different Wait Time is required move the cursor to   the 
Fixed  Retry  Wait data field and change the time using the  Up/Down  arrow 
keys.   The  range  can be from 0.0 seconds to 16.9 seconds in  0.4  second 
steps.
.P
TX TONE DURATION                                                       4723

  This is the length of each digit that is transmitted by the  radio.   The 
default is 297.0 msec.   Use the Up/Down arrow keys to change the default 
value.  The range is 66.0 to 4296 msec.  in 16.8 msec. intervals.
.P

TX TONE INTERVAL                                                       4724

  This is the time between the transmission of different DTMF digits.   The 
default  is 66.0 msec.   Use the Up/Down arrow keys to change the  default 
value.  The range is 49.7 to 4286 msec. in 16.5 msec. intervals.
.P
ACKNOWLEDGE                                                            4725

If Acknowledge is enabled, then the radio will transmit a transpond signal
when it receives the correct ID.
.P
ACCESS CODE                                                            4726

The Access Code is a string of up to 8 DTMF digits .  When DTMF/PHONE access
is assigned to a mode, the MEM * sequence will send this Access Code.
.P
DEACCESS CODE                                                          4727

The Deaccess Code is a string of up to 8 DTMF digits.  When DTMF/PHONE
access is assigned to a mode, the MEM # sequence will send this Deaccess
Code.
.P
SIDETONE                                                               4728

The Sidetone is a continuous tone heard from  PTT until the ID has been sent.

Sidetones are heard through the speaker when the Access and Deaccess Codes
are being transmitted.  They are used to tell the operator that he cannot 
yet speak until the ID has been transmitted.

.P
SHORT SIDETONE                                                         4729

The Short Sidetone is a short beep after the ID has been sent.

Sidetones are heard through the speaker when the Access and Deaccess Codes
are being transmitted.  They are used to tell the operator that he cannot
yet speak until the ID has been transmitted.
.P
TX HOLD TIME                                                           4730

The Tx Hold Time determines how long the radio will remain keyed between
keypad presses.  This allows the transmitter to stay up when using the DTMF
keypad for "live" dialing.  The minimum value for this field is determined
by the values of Tx Tone Duration and Tx Tone Interval.  The Tx Hold Time
CANNOT be less than the sum of the two Tx Tone values.  The maximum value
for this field is 4.2 seconds and the default is 2.5 seconds.
.P
OVERRIDE BCL                                                           4731

This  will  determine  whether or not Busy Channel Lockout  (BCL)  will  be 
defeated  while in phone mode. If "Y", then BCL will be defeated  while  in 
phone mode. If "N", then BCL will not be defeated while in phone mode.
.P
PTT DISABLES SIG SQUELCH                                               4732

This indicates whether signalling squelch will be disabled following an
internal or external PTT press.
.P
HORN/LIGHTS DURATION                                                   4733

This specifies the length of time the external alarm is activated. Use the
UP/DOWN arrow keys to specify either 5.0, 10.0, 15.0, 20.0, 25.0, or 30.0
seconds.
.P
GENERIC BYTES                                                          4734

Custom bytes that are used to configure thrid-party full serial option
boards. There are a maximum of 21 bytes available.

Refer to third party option board instructions for specific details.
.P
IMPORT GENERIC SIGNALLING FILE                                         4790

The IMPORT GENERIC SIGNALLING FILE function is used to retrieve a
generic signalling file from a diskette or hard disk.  Generic signalling
files can be created by using SMRTRNK2.EXE, a utility distributed with 
the RSS.  These files are denoted by the *.FRE suffix.

The list of generic signalling files found in the specified pathname will
be displayed upon entering this screen.  Use the TAB (or ENTER) key to 
select the desired file to import.  If the the pathname is incorrect, 
press F2 to change it.  Once the desired filename has been highlighted, 
press F8 to import the file.  Once imported, the file's contents will 
be loaded into the current GENERIC signalling system.                          

The functions available from this screen are described in subsequent
pages.
.P
IMPORT GENERIC SIGNALLING FILE (cont)

Function Key Descriptions:

F1 - Displays on-line help.

F2 - The CHANGE PATH function is used to specify the directory path where
     the generic signalling file is to be located.  The default path will
     be the specified Archive Path from the SETUP Menu (See MAIN MENU,
     F9).

F5 - The PRINT SCREEN function prints an exact copy of the current screen.
.P
IMPORT GENERIC SIGNALLING FILE (cont)

F8 - The IMPORT FILE functions reads the highlighted filename and loads
     the contents into the current GENERIC signalling system.  Once the
     file has been imported, the RSS will returned to the GENERIC 
     signalling system screen, where the user can return to editing.

F10 - Exits the current screen.
.P
PATH                                                                  4791

Enter the hard disk or floppy pathname where generic signalling files are
to be located.  Generic signalling files are denoted by the *.FRE suffix.

.P
